```json
[
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/properties/index.py",
    "line": 493,
    "summary": "GitTree.paths() and GitTree.read() use different path bases when `root` is a subdirectory of the repo.",
    "failure_scenario": "`git ls-tree -r` run via `git -C <subdir>` lists only entries under that subdirectory, with paths relative to it. I confirmed this in this repo with `git -C src/forseti ls-tree` (it prints `__init__.py`). `git cat-file blob <sha>:<path>` resolves `<path>` from the repo root. For a monorepo `GitTree.at(Path('repo/subproject'))`, ProjectIndex gets paths like `src/ring.c`, and `read('src/ring.c')` then fails with 'path does not exist', raised as ProjectIndexError. Use `--full-tree` and a root-relative scheme, or resolve the toplevel and the prefix. The tests only ever use the repo root, so this is not covered."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/properties/index.py",
    "line": 628,
    "summary": "`find` and `call_sites` do not respect linkage: a same-named `static` function in an unrelated .c file shadows the real callee, and its calls are attributed as callers.",
    "failure_scenario": "Unit `a.c::f` calls `helper`, which is declared in a header and defined extern in `z.c`. `b.c` has `static int helper(...)`. In `find`, a prototype in the origin file only sets `found`, and the loop then returns the first definition in sorted path order, which is `b.c`'s static. The callee slice then shows the wrong signature, doc comment and contracts (the unit id is keyed to `b.c`). The reverse also happens in `_call_entries`: for an extern target, `b.c`'s own `h()` calls to its static `h` are listed under 'how it is called'. Names such as `init`, `probe` and `show` are heavily duplicated in kernel-style C. Skip other files' file-local statics (`is_file_local` already exists) when resolving across files."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/properties/index.py",
    "line": 623,
    "summary": "`is_file_local` searches for `static` in the whole function head, including the parameter list, so the C99 `int f(int a[static 3])` form is treated as a file-local function.",
    "failure_scenario": "An extern function in a .c file uses `int sum(const int a[static 4])`. `_STATIC_RE` matches inside the parentheses, so `is_file_local` returns True. `_call_entries` then forces `Reach.FILE`, and every caller and test in other files is dropped from the pack even though the function is public. Only the declaration specifiers before the first top-level `(` should be searched."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/properties/index.py",
    "line": 684,
    "summary": "`Scan.definitions()` is recomputed for every file on every `definitions_used` call, and the index re-scans the whole project per pack under the default `Reach.PROJECT`.",
    "failure_scenario": "`ProjectIndex` caches sources, scans, refs and call sites, but `self.scan(path).definitions()` is not cached. Each `build_context_pack` therefore reruns `_type_names`, `_enumerators` and the define regexes over every file in the project to build `by_name`. At project scale (the RFC-0007 kernel target) with many units, this is O(project) of pure-Python work per unit. Memoise `definitions()` on Scan, or cache the `by_name` map per (origin, reach) on the index."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/properties/index.py",
    "line": 496,
    "summary": "GitTree.read spawns one `git cat-file` subprocess per file, and `Reach.PROJECT` (the default) reads and scans every .c/.h file in the tree.",
    "failure_scenario": "`call_sites` is called for every file in reach for both the CALLERS and TESTS slices, and `find` and `definitions_used` also scan every file. On a large repo this means tens of thousands of process spawns before the first pack is returned. Use one long-lived `git cat-file --batch` process, or `git archive`/`git grep` prefiltering, so only files containing the target name are read."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/properties/pack.py",
    "line": 866,
    "summary": "The `contracts` seam is not pinned to the commit, and `store_contracts` keys on the index's normalised path, so it can leak later knowledge or silently miss.",
    "failure_scenario": "The module docstring claims 'because the index reads one commit nothing written after it can leak in'. `store_contracts` reads currently ACCEPTED properties from the live store with no commit or timestamp bound, so a contract accepted after the pinned commit (for example one derived from the fix) appears under 'accepted:' in a historical pack. It also looks up `make_unit_id(callee.path, name)`, using the tree-relative path. Store ids written with Core's raw path spelling (absolute or `./`-prefixed, per the unit_id docs) never match, and the pack drops contracts without any error."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/properties/pack.py",
    "line": 974,
    "summary": "A `static` target is restricted to its own file for the TESTS slice too, so unit tests that `#include` the .c file are never found.",
    "failure_scenario": "A static function `advance` in `ring.c` is exercised by `tests/test_ring.c`, which does `#include \"../src/ring.c\"` (the usual way to unit-test static functions, and the kunit pattern). `reach = Reach.FILE` is applied to the TESTS slice as well, so the test file is skipped and the 'tests that call it' slice comes back empty. Test files should keep project reach, or at least files that include the target's file."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/properties/index.py",
    "line": 606,
    "summary": "Top-level macro invocations ending in `;` are recorded as function prototypes.",
    "failure_scenario": "`EXPORT_SYMBOL(rb_push);`, `MODULE_LICENSE(\"GPL\");`, `module_init(foo_init);` and `static DEFINE_MUTEX(m);` all pass the `endswith(';')` and `_trailing_call_name` checks. They are added to `functions()` as prototypes named EXPORT_SYMBOL, DEFINE_MUTEX and so on. `find()` can then resolve a body macro call of the same name (for example a macro used both at top level and in functions) as a 'callee' with garbage signature text. The macro invocation also appears as a bogus function ref in the siblings path of `functions()` consumers. Filter prototypes that have no return-type specifier before the name."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/properties/index.py",
    "line": 563,
    "summary": "`self.files` is a tuple, so every membership test (`source`, `definition`) is O(number of files).",
    "failure_scenario": "`definition()` and the uncached branch of `source()` do `path in self.files` on a tuple. `_call_entries` calls `definition()` per caller, and `source()` is hit for every file in reach, so a 30k-file project gives O(n^2) behaviour. Keep a `frozenset` alongside the sorted tuple."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/properties/pack.py",
    "line": 1060,
    "summary": "Type depth is derived from `SliceKind.TARGET in kinds`, so a pack with neither TARGET nor SIGNATURE silently uses signature-only types. This contradicts the docstring 'Target ... Always included'.",
    "failure_scenario": "`PackConfig(slices={TYPES})` (used in `test_examples_pack_includes_types`) computes types from the signature only, so types used only in the body (for example a local `struct tmp`, or macros like CAP used inside the function) are missing. The same config with TARGET added gives a different type set. Nothing in the docs says a body-less pack is blind by default. The 'always included' target claim is also false for configs such as `{CALLERS}`. Either always include the target or document and validate the rule."
  }
]
